.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt DTRACED 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dtraced
.Nd DTrace hypervisor management daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm dtraced
.Op Fl OZdhmqv
.Op Fl D Ar directory
.Op Fl K Ar private-key.pem Fl P Ar peer-key.pem
.Op Fl t Ar size
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a system daemon responsible for coordinating DTrace-based tracing across
virtualization hosts and guests.
This is required for hypervisor-based tracing where scripts execute in the
host kernel, but probes are instrumented and fire from some combination of the
host and guest kernels.
On the host,
.Nm
receives connections from local
.Xr dtrace 1
clients, loading DTrace scripts and distributing suitably linked versions of
scripts to all affected guests.
On the guest side,
.Nm
receives DTrace scripts provided by the host and installs them so that
suitable guest probes fire.
.Nm
currently supports only the
.Xr bhyve 8
hypervisor.
.Pp
The following options are available:
.Bl -tag -width flag
.It Fl D Ar directory
Specify the
.Nm
base directory, where scripts will be stored during execution.
The directory must be an absolute path.
The base directory defaults to
.Pa /var/ddtrace .
.It Fl O
Run
.Nm
in overlord mode, overriding automatic detection of being a hypervisor host.
.It Fl K Ar private-key.pem
Load the host's Ed25519 private key for HyperTrace source-release attestations.
The file must be an absolute-path, single-link regular PEM file owned by the
daemon's effective user, with no group or other permissions.
Requires
.Fl P
and overlord mode.
.It Fl P Ar peer-key.pem
Pin the intended peer host's Ed25519 public key for HyperTrace handoffs.
The absolute-path, single-link regular PEM file must be owned by the daemon's
effective user and not writable by group or others.
Requires
.Fl K
and overlord mode.
These keys bind a signed source-release record to one trusted peer; they do
not by themselves authorize cross-host restore.
.It Fl Z
Do not prefix programs sent to consumers with their SHA256 digest.
Programs sent to forwarders (a
.Xr bhyve 8
virtio-dtrace device on the host, the transport forwarder in a guest) are
always bare ELF objects, optionally preceded by a VM target envelope; the
device refuses any other framing.
The digest detects integrity issues; it does not restore an interrupted
tracing session.
This flag exists for debugging purposes only.
.It Fl d
Debug mode.
The server sends verbose debug output to standard error, and does not put
itself in the background.
.It Fl h
Print a help message and terminate.
.It Fl m
Run
.Nm
in minion mode, overriding automatic detection of being a hypervisor guest.
.It Fl q
Enable quiet mode, suppressing print of status updates and errors on the
terminal.
.It Fl t Ar size
Set the worker count, from 1 through 1024.
The default is 1.
.It Fl v
Display version information.
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION
Set
.Va dtraced_enable="YES"
in
.Xr rc.conf 5
to enable startup.
.Va dtraced_type
accepts
.Li auto
(the default),
.Li overlord
(host), or
.Li minion
(guest).
Additional command-line options may be supplied in
.Va dtraced_flags .
The guest requires the
.Pa /dev/dttransport
device before startup.
On a host, bhyve forwarders pass a VM descriptor restricted to the identity
ioctl during the control-socket handshake.
.Nm
checks the kernel VM name, ID, and epoch before accepting a forwarder and
checks that the epoch remains current while forwarding.
Installation and terminal reports must name that same VM lifetime.
The terminal report also covers a delivered program that did not install.
Up to four connection handshakes proceed concurrently; a full admission pool
can delay new connections until a handshake completes or times out.
Targeted programs and revocations are sent only to their named VM lifetime.
Changing
.Fl D
does not change the control socket path,
.Pa /var/ddtrace/sub.sock .
.Sh SECURITY
.Nm
and its consumers are traditional privileged DTrace tools.
Access to the control socket is limited by the permissions of
.Pa /var/ddtrace
(mode 0700, owned by root).
A
.Xr dtrace 1
consumer that submits programs identifies itself by its process ID and its
kernel process birth time, which
.Nm
checks against the socket peer credentials.
libdtrace obtains that birth identity with
.Xr pdopenpid 2
when the consumer runs with ambient authority, as
.Xr dtrace 1
does from a shell, and with
.Xr pdself 2 ,
which is permitted only in capability mode, otherwise.
.Pp
With the 5BSD capability plane enabled,
.Nm ,
.Xr dtrace 1
in its HyperTrace modes,
.Xr dtracedctl 8
and the
.Xr bhyve 8
virtio-dtrace backend are not clients of
.Xr BSDTrace 8 .
They do not use its consumer descriptor: the overlord uses
.Pa /dev/dtrace/hypertrace
and VM attestation descriptors, a minion uses
.Pa /dev/dttransport ,
and HyperTrace consumers need DTrace ioctls outside the set that
.Xr BSDTrace 8
delegates.
.Xr BSDTrace 8
does not reserve
.Pa /dev/dtrace/dtrace ,
so it does not block these tools.
Their authority is root's, granted by device and directory permissions.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtrace 1 ,
.Xr pdopenpid 2 ,
.Xr pdself 2 ,
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr bhyve 8 ,
.Xr BSDTrace 8 ,
.Xr dtracedctl 8
.Rs
